<!-- Real-world purchase order (cXML 1.1, captured 2026-07-22), de-identified for the repo:
     every trading-party name, network identity, postal address, site code and customer id is a
     placeholder. Item codes, quantities, currency and money are VERBATIM — they are the test signal,
     and nothing in this file that carries them identifies anybody.

     Why this fixture exists: here the buyer's <SupplierPartID> and the <ManufacturerPartID> are the
     SAME string ("REDACTED-ORDER-DATA"). This is the easy case that made the old blind "suggest the MPN as
     the supplier code" shortcut look correct. Pair it with
     real-cxml-1.2-ariba-punchout-mpn-differs.xml, where they are completely different, to see why
     manufacturer part number has to be a real lookup key.

     Shape that is deliberately preserved: a <!DOCTYPE> header, a <Street> line that crams a company
     name and the whole address into one element, and a ";"-delimited <Street> on the BillTo side.
     Both are real formatting habits of cXML 1.1 senders and both must survive parsing. -->
